{"step_index":156,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-16T17:32:04Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-16T23:02:04+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-16T23:02:10+05:30\nThe search for \"\"python\" \"SVG\" \"drawing\" \"video\" \"ffmpeg\" github script\" returned the following summary:\nTo create a video from SVG drawings using Python and FFmpeg, you typically follow a two-step process: generating individual frames (as raster images like PNGs) and then encoding those frames into a video file using FFmpeg.\n\nBelow are the most relevant GitHub resources and approaches for this task:\n\n### 1. Dedicated Python Projects on GitHub\nSeveral projects specifically handle the conversion of SVG files or animations into video:\n\n*   **[andchir/svg_to_video](https://github.com/andchir/svg_to_video)**: A Python-based tool designed to convert animated SVGs to video files. It uses libraries like `CairoSVG` and `moviepy` to process the SVGs and `ffmpeg` to encode the final output[1][2].\n*   **[apstrinka/svg-to-video](https://github.com/apstrinka/svg-to-video)**: A straightforward Python script that requires `cairosvg` and `ffmpeg`. It is specifically built to turn animated SVGs into MP4s[3][4].\n*   **[fpermana/svgbuild-py3](https://github.com/fpermana/svgbuild-py3)**: A Python 3 library that helps create a sequence of images from SVG files, which can then be combined into a video using FFmpeg[5].\n\n### 2. Programmatic SVG Generation (Python)\nIf you need to *generate* the SVG content programmatically before turning it into a video, these libraries are the industry standard:\n\n*   **[cduck/drawsvg](https://github.com/cduck/drawsvg)**: A powerful Python 3 library for programmatically generating SVG images and animations. It includes features to render drawings and is well-suited for creating frame sequences for video[6][7].\n\n### 3. The General Workflow\nIf you prefer to build your own pipeline, the most robust method follows these steps:\n\n1.  **Generate Frames**: Use a Python library (like `drawsvg` or `cairosvg`) to export each \"frame\" of your animation as a PNG file[5][8][9].\n2.  **Naming Convention**: Ensure your images are saved with sequential naming (e.g., `frame_001.png`, `frame_002.png`)[10].\n3.  **FFmpeg Encoding**: Run an FFmpeg command via Pythonâ€™s `subprocess` module to stitch the frames into a video[11][10]:\n    ```bash\n    ffmpeg -framerate 30 -i frame_%03d.png -c:v libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p output.mp4\n    ```\n\n### Alternative Approaches\n*   **Node.js / Puppeteer**: For complex SMIL or CSS-based SVG animations, many developers prefer using **[jcubic/svg-video](https://github.com/jcubic/svg-video)** or **[freder/svg-animation-to-video](https://github.com/freder/svg-animation-to-video)**. These tools use a headless browser (Puppeteer) to accurately capture animations that are difficult to render with standard Python image libraries[12][13][14].\n*   **FFmpeg `drawvg`**: For advanced users, there is a specific FFmpeg filter called `drawvg` (available in specialized branches) that allows you to render vector graphics directly within the FFmpeg processing pipeline using a Vector Graphics Script (VGS)[15].\n\nSources:\n[1] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFiBibUCSUfyaAQbuwXzlWA-1vPQWMQsUpUV5rsZXkQVWEla_jZVjBa1tN092cQWV6nRpWDtdmCLQveD5aTQ38jyszjif0lD-cyRzTWFO_AlAPpAhCpkvd_sgOw2Jo=)\n[2] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFiBibUCSUfyaAQbuwXzlWA-1vPQWMQsUpUV5rsZXkQVWEla_jZVjBa1tN092cQWV6nRpWDtdmCLQveD5aTQ38jyszjif0lD-cyRzTWFO_AlAPpAhCpkvd_sgOw2Jo=)\n[3] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQErEEaS2_1Ik7_CtLpLr-Y9VH-wzN2xNIZSlsNIUGTGHF5jls2wGf_7snffJqENg1V2_roHqlVg5gPETay_myVnCM_OFkcyTmAijyTs_U20qLDNdC7ifwROA1woUvY0Xw==)\n[4] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQErEEaS2_1Ik7_CtLpLr-Y9VH-wzN2xNIZSlsNIUGTGHF5jls2wGf