{"step_index":159,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-16T17:32:21Z","content":"Priyanshu,\n\n### ANSWER\nYou are looking specifically for the \"stroke reveal\" or line-drawing effect, rather than just having images pop up on the screen. I ran a deep research scan focused entirely on projects that automate this specific vector line-drawing animation. \n\nSince you relaxed the requirement for high GitHub stars, we can look at the utility scripts that actually get this specific job done.\n\n### WHY\n\nHere are the repositories and methods that specifically execute the stroke-by-stroke drawing effect:\n\n**1. Puppeteer / Headless Browser Recorders (Most Reliable for SVGs)**\n*   **`jcubic/svg-video`** & **`freder/svg-animation-to-video`**\n*   *How it creates the drawing effect:* Instead of doing complex math in Python, these tools use a web development trick. They load your SVG into a hidden Chrome browser (Puppeteer) and apply a CSS `stroke-dashoffset` animation. This natively forces the SVG to draw itself line-by-line. The script then uses FFmpeg to record that hidden browser tab into an MP4 video.\n\n**2. Python SVG-to-Video Generators**\n*   **`cduck/drawsvg`**: A Python 3 library that lets you programmatically code vector graphics and export them as animated frames.\n*   **`andchir/svg_to_video`**: A lightweight Python script that uses `CairoSVG` and `moviepy` to take an already animated SVG file and stitch it into a standard MP4 video.\n\n**3. The Blender \"Build Modifier\" Workflow**\n*   *How it creates the drawing effect:* You actually don't need a heavy script for this. Blender's Grease Pencil has a native tool called the **Build Modifier**. If you import an SVG into Blender and apply this modifier, Blender automatically animates the strokes appearing one by one in the exact sequence they were created. It perfectly mimics a human sketching. The previously mentioned `doakey3/GreaseWriter` add-on just automates this process specifically for handwriting text.\n\n### COUNTER\n\nEven if you accept low-star repositories that successfully create the drawing effect, you still face a critical visual problem. \n\nThe scripts above will perfectly animate the lines of your SVG. However, if you download a free stock SVG of a person from Freepik, the computer has no idea what a \"face\" or an \"arm\" is. It will just draw the lines in whatever random order the original artist created the vector layers. It might draw the left shoe, then jump to an eyeball, then jump to the shirt, then back to the right shoe. \n\nIt will not look like a human sketching a story; it will look like a computer glitching out while trying to print a file.\n\n### CONDITIONS\n\nIf you want to use these CLI drawing tools and actually have them look like a professional explainer video, you MUST open your downloaded SVGs in a program like Adobe Illustrator or Inkscape first. \n\nYou have to manually reorganize the paths so that the outline draws first, the body second, and the details last. If you are willing to do that manual prep work for your graphics, then `jcubic/svg-video` (via Node.js) or Blender's native Build Modifier will give you the exact line-drawing effect you want, entirely for free.","thinking":"**Clarifying Implementation Strategies**\n\nThe research confirms my initial assumptions, but brings into focus an implementation detail. It looks like the right way to approach drawing programmatically involves the `andchir/svg_to_video` library.\n\n\n**Synthesizing Drawing Tools**\n\nI've narrowed the focus to tools that emphasize the \"drawing\" effect explicitly. Initial investigation into cduck/drawsvg for programmatic vector generation continues. However, I'm now leaning toward Puppeteer/JS solutions, specifically jcubic/svg-video or freder/svg-animation-to-video, as they directly address stroke-dashoffset animation. Blender's Grease Pencil with the Build Modifier is another promising option, given its native support for stroke-by-stroke reveal. I will disregard the stars parameter and focus on actual implementation of the drawing animation.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Vector Animation**\n\nI've been drilling down on projects that automate the vector line-drawing. Forget the stars; it's all about direct implementation. The Puppeteer solutions, like `jcubic/svg-video` and `freder/svg-animation-to-video`, are standing out because they use `stroke-dashoffset`, which achieves the desired effect. `cduck/drawsvg` is a viable alternative if I want to code the drawings from scratch.\n\n\n**Evaluating Drawing Order**\n\nI'm now fully immersed in the problem of line drawing order. The \"Build Modifier\" in Blender's Grease Pencil appears ideal; it handles the animation directly. However, it looks like even with tools like Python SVG tracers, the core issue remains: these tools don't inherently understand the logical drawing order of shapes in an SVG, and the resulting animation will likely be disjointed and arbitrary. It seems like the line ordering must be manually sorted.\n\n\n"}
